        <div class="card">
            <h2>Understanding Algorithmic Engagement</h2>
            <p>Modern social media platforms are powered by complex recommendation algorithms designed to maximize user engagement and retention. By analyzing watch time, likes, and interaction patterns, these algorithms curate personalized feeds that keep users scrolling for hours.</p>
            <p>While platforms promote connection, excessive or problematic use can lead to a "delusional parallel reality" where teens become isolated behind screens rather than engaging in meaningful community.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
            <h2>Common Screen Time Myths vs. Scientific Facts</h2>
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Myth:</strong> All screen time has the exact same psychological impact on teenagers. <br>
                    <strong>Fact:</strong> Research distinguishes between passive consumption (endless scrolling) and active creation or social communication. Active connection with real-life peers has vastly different health outcomes than passive algorithmic consumption.</li>
                <li><strong>Myth:</strong> Social media is solely responsible for adolescent anxiety epidemics. <br>
                    <strong>Fact:</strong> While studies show correlation with sleep disruption and anxiety, mental health is multi-factorial. Digital tools amplify existing vulnerabilities when boundaries are absent.</li>
                <li><strong>Myth:</strong> Online connections are just as fulfilling as face-to-face interactions. <br>
                    <strong>Fact:</strong> Psychological studies emphasize that non-verbal cues, physical presence, and deep dialogue are essential for human empathy—elements often missing in pure digital exchanges.</li>
            </ul>

            <p>To evaluate these claims accurately, we apply the <strong>CRAAP test</strong> (Currency, Relevance, Authority, Accuracy, and Purpose) to ensure our research relies on peer-reviewed science rather than popular alarmist headlines.</p>

